# Exercise 2: The Orange Juice Problem Continues

Let us continue with the orange juice problem from **DSCI 561**'s `lab4`. Nevertheless, we will skip all the stages you executed back then (except for the *data modelling framework* and *estimation*) and dig into *model selection*. Recall the description of the data set `OJ` from package `{ISLR2}` (James et al., 2022):

> *"The data contains 1070 purchases where the customer either purchased Citrus Hill or Minute Maid Orange Juice. A number of characteristics of the customer and product are recorded."*

Function `str()` allows us to take a quick look at the nature of this dataset's variables:

```{r}
str(OJ)
```

Note our variables of interest will be the following:

- `Purchase:` The response of interest indicating whether the customer purchased Minute Maid (`MM`) or Citrus Hill (`CH`).
- `PriceDiff`: A continuous explanatory variable (or feature), the difference in juice price between both brands at purchase (`SalePriceMM - SalePriceCH`).
- `STORE`: A label indicating at what possible store a given juice was purchased. It is originally a numeric-type column (`0`, `1`, `2`, `3`, or `4`), but we will convert it to a **nominal factor** (i.e., the order of the levels does not matter) with the below code.
- `PctDiscCH`: Percentage discount for `CH` (it is on a proportion scale).
- `PctDiscMM`: Percentage discount for `MM` (it is on a proportion scale).
- `LoyalCH`: Customer brand loyalty for `CH` (an index between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates no brand loyalty and 1 complete brand loyalty).

```{r}
# Run this code before proceeding.
# It selects the columns of interest. Then, it makes STORE a factor-type variable.
training_OJ <- OJ |>
  dplyr::select(Purchase, PriceDiff, STORE, PctDiscCH, PctDiscMM, LoyalCH) |>
  mutate(STORE = as.factor(STORE)) 
```

## Main Statistical Inquiries

Suppose you are part of the Data Science team from **Citrus Hill**. Via the dataset `training_OJ` and the previous variables, you are interested in fitting a model that best fits this training data so you can use the corresponding modelling estimates to predict the probability of any given future customer purchasing Citrus Hill. Note you will not have any further testing data in this situation.

Specifically, your response of interest is the following:

$$
Y_i =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the $i$th customer purchased Citrus Hill},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

## Q2.1. Data Modelling Framework
  
rubric={reasoning:11}

Given the above response definition, let us set up an adequate data modelling framework. Firstly, for $i = 1, \dots, n$, let us mathematically define the response of interest in our training set of size $n$. We will assume the $i$th response as:
  
$$Y_i \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_i),$$
  
where $p_i$ is the Citrus Hill purchasing probability of the $i$th customer.

Now, suppose your team has proposed the following five candidate models:
  
- **Model 1.** A given regression model with `PriceDiff` as a **standalone** regressor.
- **Model 2.** A given regression model with `PriceDiff` and `STORE` as **standalone** regressors.
- **Model 3.** A given regression model with `PriceDiff`, `STORE`, and `PctDiscCH` as **standalone** regressors.
- **Model 4.** A given regression model with `PriceDiff`, `STORE`, `PctDiscCH`, and `PctDiscMM` as **standalone** regressors.
- **Model 5.** A given regression model with `PriceDiff`, `STORE`, `PctDiscCH`, `PctDiscMM`, and `LoyalCH` as **standalone** regressors.

> **Heads-up:** The term **standalone** refers to regressors that are not interacting with another one in the right-hand side of the modelling equation.

Now, answer the following:
  
a. Having defined the response (and its distributional assumption) and regressors in each candidate model, what is a suitable regression model?
b. What is the training sample's modelling equation for each of the five candidate models? To earn full marks on this question, define the necessary mathematical notation this exercise has not defined beforehand.

> **Note:** You do not need to use LaTeX to show your work when necessary. That said, you could only embed pictures of your written solution into the `.pdf`. Nevertheless, it is advisable to use LaTeX so you can practice equation writing in reproducible documents. 

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q2-1-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

> a. *Since we have Bernoulli trials in this problem, the suitable modelling choice would be a GLM such as Binary Logistic regression.*
> b. *Each candidate model will need a link function $\mbox{logit}(p_i)$ on the left-hand side with its corresponding set of regressors on the right-hand side.*

>> *For **Model 1**, let $X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i}$ be the observed price difference between both brands for the $i$th customer, thus:*

$$
\textbf{Model 1:} \quad \mbox{logit}(p_i) = \log\left(\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i}.
$$

>> *Now, besides $X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i}$, **Model 2** will include the store (a nominal factor) as a regressor. This addition will require the use of indicator variables. Assuming `STORE 0` is the baseline (for $j = 1, 2, 3, 4$), the following indicator variables will be included:*

$$
X_{{\texttt{Store}_j}_i} =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \mbox{if the $i$th customer purchased the orange juice at \texttt{STORE j}},\\
0 \; \; \; \;   \mbox{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
$$

>> *This arrangement yields the following sample's modelling equation:*
  
$$
  \textbf{Model 2:} \quad \mbox{logit}(p_i) = \log\left(\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i}\right) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i} + \beta_2 X_{{\texttt{Store}_1}_i} + \beta_3 X_{{\texttt{Store}_2}_i} + \beta_4 X_{{\texttt{Store}_3}_i} + \beta_5 X_{{\texttt{Store}_4}_i}.
$$
  
>> *For **Model 3**, we add $X_{\texttt{PctDiscCH}_i}$ as the $i$th percentage discount for `CH`:*
  
\begin{align*}
\textbf{Model 3} \quad \mbox{logit}(p_i) = \log\left(\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i}\right) &= \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i} + \beta_2 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_1}_i} + \beta_3 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_2}_i} + \beta_4 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_3}_i} + \beta_5 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_4}_i} + \\
& \qquad \beta_6 X_{\texttt{PctDiscCH}_i}.
\end{align*}

>> *In terms of **Model 4**, we add $X_{\texttt{PctDiscMM}_i}$ as the $i$th percentage discount for `MM`:*
  
\begin{align*}
\textbf{Model 4} \quad \mbox{logit}(p_i) = \log\left(\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i}\right) &= \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i} + \beta_2 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_1}_i} + \beta_3 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_2}_i} + \beta_4 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_3}_i} + \beta_5 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_4}_i} + \\
& \qquad \beta_6 X_{\texttt{PctDiscCH}_i} + \beta_7 X_{\texttt{PctDiscMM}_i}.
\end{align*}

>> *Finally, **Model 5**, will include $X_{\texttt{LoyalCH}_i}$ as the $i$th customer brand loyalty for `CH`:*
  
\begin{align*}
\textbf{Model 5} \quad \mbox{logit}(p_i) = \log\left(\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i}\right) &= \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{\texttt{PriceDiff}_i} + \beta_2 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_1}_i} + \beta_3 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_2}_i} + \beta_4 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_3}_i} + \beta_5 X_{{\texttt{STORE}_4}_i} + \\
& \qquad \beta_6 X_{\texttt{PctDiscCH}_i} + \beta_7 X_{\texttt{PctDiscMM}_i} + \beta_8 X_{\texttt{LoyalCH}_i}.
\end{align*}.

>> *Note $\beta_0, \beta_1, ..., \beta_8$ are the corresponding regression terms to be estimated.*
  
