# Exercise 5: Maximum Likelihood Estimation in Binary Logistic Regression

In [**DSCI 551's `lecture7`**](https://pages.github.ubc.ca/mds-2025-26/DSCI_551_stat-prob-dsci_students/notes/07_lecture-maximum-likelihood-estimation.html), you explored univariate maximum likelihood estimation (MLE). Now, let us expand this approach to a GLM.

Let us suppose we aim to estimate the **regression coefficients** (also called **weights** in Machine Learning) in a **Binary Logistic regression model**. Then, under this framework, MLE becomes crucial to estimate these coefficients and is the basis of the so-called `glm()` function. That said, when we have more than one regression term to estimate, MLE becomes **multivariate**.

Therefore, this exercise will introduce you to the foundations of multivariate MLE for regression terms in a specific GLM (such as the Binary Logistic regression) using analytical steps along with the principle of the link function.

> **Note:** You do not need to use LaTeX to show your work when necessary. That said, you could only embed pictures of your written solution into the `.pdf`. Nevertheless, it is advisable to use LaTeX so you can practice equation writing in reproducible documents. 

## (Challenging) Q5.1. Response's Distributional Assumption

rubric={reasoning:1}

Suppose you have a **training dataset** of size $n$ with the $i$th response $Y_i$ subject to $k$ regressors $X_{i, 1}, \dots, X_{i, k}$ ($i = 1, \dots, n$). **Under this framework, the regressors can take a continuous or discrete nature.**

Now, let us begin with **step 1**: *choosing the right response distribution*. In Binary Logistic regression, the response takes on the following values:

$$
Y_i =
\begin{cases}
1 \; \; \; \; \text{if there is a success},\\
0 \; \; \; \; \mbox{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$

The **most basic** Binary Logistic regression model assumes that the $n$ $Y_i$s are **independent only** (not identically distributed!). Given this assumption, what theoretical distribution can we assume for $Y_i$ to perform MLE? Why are you choosing this distribution along with its parameter(s)? **Answer in two or three sentences.**

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q5-1-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

> *The $n$ responses $Y_i$ can be assumed as Bernoulli trials given their binary nature. Nevertheless, since they are only independent, each one will have its probability of success $p_i \in [0, 1]$. Therefore:*

$$Y_i \sim \text{Bernoulli}(p_i).$$

<!-- # END SOLUTION -->

<!-- # END QUESTION -->

## (Challenging) Q5.2. Joint Probability Mass Function

rubric={reasoning:1}

Using your answer from **Q5.1**, execute **step 2**: *obtaining the joint probability mass function (PMF)*. You will need the corresponding PMFs of the $n$ discrete $Y_i$s. [**DSCI 551's distribution cheatsheet**](https://pages.github.ubc.ca/mds-2025-26/DSCI_551_stat-prob-dsci_students/notes/appendix-dist-cheatsheet.html) would be useful here.

Recall that the notation on the right-hand side for random variables changes to lower cases since we depict $n$ **observed values**.

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q5-2-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

$$P_{Y_1, \dots, Y_n} \left(Y_1 = y_1, \dots, Y_n = y_n \mid p_1, \dots, p_n \right) = \prod_{i = 1}^n p_i^{y_i} (1 - p_i)^{1 - y_i}.$$

<!-- # END SOLUTION -->

<!-- # END QUESTION -->

## (Challenging) Q5.3. Likelihood Function

rubric={reasoning:1}

Using your answer from **Q5.2**, execute **step 3**: *obtaining the likelihood function*.

$$l(p_1, \dots, p_n \mid y_1, \dots , y_{n}).$$ 

**Briefly**, justify your answer.

> **Hint:** [**This section**](https://pages.github.ubc.ca/mds-2025-26/DSCI_551_stat-prob-dsci_students/notes/07_lecture-maximum-likelihood-estimation.html#but-what-is-the-likelihood-function) from DSCI 551's `lecture7` might be helpful.

