## (Challenging) Q5.4. Link Function

rubric={reasoning:1}

Let $\beta_0$ be the regression intercept along with the $k$ regression coefficients $\beta_1, \dots, \beta_k$ corresponding to the $k$ regressors (also known as features) $X_{i, 1}, \dots, X_{i, k}$. Unlike OLS, we cannot directly relate the $i$th response $Y_i$ with $\beta_0, \beta_1, \dots, \beta_k$ and the $k$ regressors.

Let $p_i$ be the probability of success in $Y_i$. We know that Binary Logistic regression will need to use a **link function** $h(p_i)$ with the above regression terms. Specifically, we do it via the natural logarithm (i.e., on the base $e$) of the odds $\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i}$:

$$h(p_i) = \mbox{logit}(p_i) = \log \left( \frac{p_i}{1 - p_i} \right) = \beta_0 + \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j}.$$

Hence, using this equation

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:log_odds}
\log \left( \frac{p_i}{1 - p_i} \right) = \beta_0 + \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j},
\end{equation}

show that $h(p_i)$ is mononotic; i.e.,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Q1_4_function}
p_i = \frac{1}{1 + \exp \left( -\beta_0 - \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right)}.
\end{equation}

<!--
# BEGIN QUESTION
name: Q5-4-reasoning
manual: true
-->

**ANSWER:**

<!-- # BEGIN SOLUTION -->

\begin{gather*}
\log \left( \frac{p_i}{1 - p_i} \right) = \beta_0 + \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \\
\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i} = \exp \left( \beta_0 + \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right) \\
\frac{p_i}{1 - p_i} = \frac{1}{\exp \left( - \beta_0 - \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right)} \\
p_i \exp \left( - \beta_0 - \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right) = 1 - p_i \\
p_i + p_i \exp \left( - \beta_0 - \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right) = 1 \\
p_i \left[ 1 + \exp \left( - \beta_0 - \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right) \right] = 1 \\
p_i = \frac{1}{1 + \exp \left( - \beta_0 - \sum_{j = 1}^k \beta_j X_{i, j} \right)}.
\end{gather*}

<!-- # END SOLUTION -->

<!-- # END QUESTION -->

